//! THE PIPE: `x |> f(a)` IS `f(x, a)` — application, and nothing
//! else. The parse desugars into the call it names, so no pass
//! learns a node: typing, both engines and every idiom rule read an
//! ordinary call, and only the printer reads the mark that spells it
//! back.
//!
//! A STAGE IS A CALL, and the value becomes its FIRST argument:
//!
//!     x |> f              f(x)
//!     x |> f(a, k: v)     f(x, a, k: v)
//!     x |> f<T>(a)        f<T>(x, a)
//!     x |> f { … }        f(x) { … }
//!     x |> a.m            a.m(x)
//!     x |> a.m(b)         a.m(x, b)
//!     x |> (g)(b)         (g)(x, b)
//!     x |> ((v) -> …)     ((v) -> …)(x)
//!     x |> f(a)?          f(x, a)?            `!` alike
//!
//! Anything else is no stage and is refused; a method on the value
//! itself stays `x.m()`. The value is SEATED AS A WRITTEN ARGUMENT
//! IS — a `mut` first seat is written through, and a named argument
//! or a default fills the seats after it.
//!
//! A STAGE BINDS THE PRONOUN AS A METHOD CALL DOES: `it` in a stage's
//! own arguments is that stage's — `users |> filter(it.age >= 18) |>
//! map(it.name)` reads as `users.filter(…).map(…)` would. The nearest
//! call still wins: a stage inside a method's argument takes its own
//! `it`, and a plain call inside a stage's argument (`xs |> map(g(it))`)
//! leaves it to the stage. The piped VALUE is not an argument of the
//! stage: `it |> f` reads the pronoun of the call around it.
//!
//! PRECEDENCE, loosest to tightest — the pipe sits between `??` and
//! the bitwise band, and folds LEFT (`x |> f |> g` is `g(f(x))`):
//!
//!     catch  ||  &&  == != < <= > >= is  ??  |>  | ^ & << >>  + -  * / %
//!
//!     a + b |> f          f(a + b)
//!     x |> f ?? d         f(x) ?? d
//!     x |> n > 3          n(x) > 3
//!     a ?? b |> f         a ?? f(b)
//!     x |> f + 1          refused — `(x |> f) + 1`
//!
//! LINES: a line may END in `|>`. One that begins with it starts a
//! statement, as every continuing operator does, and is refused.
use core.{Expr, ExprId, BinOp, op_symbol}
use features.{LangNode, Builder}

/// `l ( |> r )*` folds left, each stage taking the value built so
/// far as its first argument.
fn build_fold_pipe(b: Builder) -> Result<LangNode, string> {
    mut acc = b.expr(0)?
    for stage in b.exprs(1)? {
        let call? = b.store.piped(stage, acc) else { return .Err(no_stage(b, stage)) }
        acc = call
    }
    b.wrap_expr(acc)
}

/// A line that begins with `|>`.
fn build_leading_pipe(_b: Builder) -> Result<LangNode, string> {
    .Err(leading_pipe())
}

// ── voices ──

/// The refusal a right side that is no call hears — an operator's
/// run names the group that pipes first.
fn no_stage(b: Builder, stage: ExprId) -> string {
    let op? = stage_operator(b, stage) else { return stage_is_a_call() }
    operator_after_stage(op_symbol(op))
}

fn stage_operator(b: Builder, stage: ExprId) -> BinOp? {
    match b.store.expr(stage) { .Binary(op, _, _) -> op, _ -> null }
}

fn stage_is_a_call() -> string {
    "a pipe's right side is a call, and the piped value becomes its first argument — `x |> f`, `x |> f(a)`, `x |> a.m(b)`, `x |> ((v) -> …)`; a method on the value itself is `x.m()`"
}

fn operator_after_stage(op: string) -> string {
    "a pipe's right side is a call, and `|>` binds looser than `${op}` — write `(x |> f) ${op} …` to pipe first"
}

fn leading_pipe() -> string {
    "a line never begins with `|>` — a continuing operator ends the line it continues, so end the previous line with `|>`"
}
